course: dictionary
lesson: 1565
title: Chapel, a small church or a place to pray
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A chapel is a small church, or a room for prayer inside a bigger building such as a school, hospital, prison, palace or airport. Read the words around it to find the meaning. «Prince Harry married Meghan Markle at St George's Chapel, Windsor» tells you that married and wedding words are near chapel, so a chapel is a place where people can hold a wedding.
explain: A chapel can also be a small part of a large church or cathedral, with its own altar. An altar is the table where prayers and services are led. So when the text says a chapel has its own altar, it is talking about a small room or corner inside a bigger church, not a whole separate building.
explain: In the UK, chapel can mean a church of some Protestant groups, such as Methodists or Baptists, especially in Wales. «We go to chapel» means the family goes to that kind of church. The word «chapel» is the same, but the clues tell you which meaning is right.
explain: Always test your meaning. Put «a small church or a room for prayer» into the sentence «Michelangelo painted the Sistine Chapel ceiling». Does it still make sense? It does, so you have found the right meaning. If it does not fit, try a different meaning.
fact: A chapel is a small church, or a room for Christian prayer inside a bigger building.
fact: «Chapel» can mean a small part of a large church or cathedral with its own altar.
fact: In Wales, «chapel» can mean the church of Methodists or Baptists: «We go to chapel.»
fact: Royal weddings have taken place in St George's Chapel at Windsor.
fact: Michelangelo painted the Sistine Chapel ceiling between 1508 and 1512, standing on a scaffold and bending his head back.
exercise: «Prince Harry married Meghan Markle at St George's Chapel, Windsor.» Using the clue married, what is a chapel likely to be? | *A small church, or a place for prayer and weddings | A kind of train station | A market selling fruit | A sports field for football
exercise: In Wales, someone says, «We go to chapel.» What does this mean? | They go to a palace for a royal party | *They go to a Protestant church, such as a Methodist or Baptist church | They go to the airport to catch a plane | They go to a famous art museum
exercise: «Michelangelo painted the Sistine Chapel ceiling standing on a scaffold and bending his head back, not lying down.» Find and copy the word that tells you how he held his head. | standing | painted | ceiling | *bending
test: «The Sistine Chapel ceiling was painted from 1508 to 1512.» How many years did the painting take? | 5 years | 10 years | 3 years | *4 years
test: A chapel is a small part of a large church or cathedral. What does it have of its own? | A bell-ringing school | *An altar | A clock shop | A football pitch
exam: «Nan is glued to the telly as Prince Harry marries Meghan Markle at St George's Chapel.» Which word is the clue that a chapel is a place for weddings? | telly | glued | *marries | Glasgow
